<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arib al-Ma'muniyya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">797–890</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as ʿArīb al-Ma’mūnīya, Arib</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arib al-Ma'muniyya was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiyan">qayna</a>, a slave trained in the arts of entertainment, of the early Abbasid period, characterised as the most famous slave singer ever to reside at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> court. Born in 797, she claimed to be the daughter of the Barmakid Ja'far ibn Yahya, a parentage modern scholars have questioned; she was bought by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a> and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>, and was freed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a>. A poet, singer, composer and oud player, she left songs said to number around 1,000, and the Kitab al-Aghani of Abu'l-Faraj al-Isfahani places her singing without rival among her peers. Her career spanned the courts of five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, and she died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> in 890, aged 96.</p></div>
